   "cite": "nonrepeated",
   "prompt": "<p>For the following problems, use the substitutions $\\tan (\\frac{x}{2})=t,$ $dx=\\frac{2}{1+t^{2}}dt,$ $\\sin x=\\frac{2t}{1+t^{2}},$ and $\\cos x=\\frac{1-t^{2}}{1+t^{2}}.$</p><p>Given $\\tan (\\frac{x}{2})=t,$ derive the formulas $dx=\\frac{2}{1+t^{2}}dt,$ $\\sin x=\\frac{2t}{1+t^{2}},$ and $\\cos x=\\frac{1-t^{2}}{1+t^{2}}.$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "solution": "From $t=\\tan (\\frac{x}{2}),$ $x=2\\;{\\tan }^{-1}t,$ so $dx=\\frac{2}{1+t^{2}}dt.$ A right triangle with legs $1$ and $t$ has the angle $\\frac{x}{2}$ opposite $t$ and hypotenuse $\\sqrt{1+t^{2}},$ so $\\sin (\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}$ and $\\cos (\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t^{2}}}.$ The double-angle formulas then give $\\sin x=2\\sin (\\frac{x}{2})\\cos (\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{2t}{1+t^{2}}$ and $\\cos x={\\cos }^{2}(\\frac{x}{2})-{\\sin }^{2}(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1-t^{2}}{1+t^{2}}.$",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
  },
